.TH CERTIFY 8
.SH NAME
certify \- create and inspect authentication certificates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B certify
[
.B \-f
]
[
.B \-k
.I keyfile
]
.B init
[
.I owner
]
.br
.B certify
.B user
.I name
[
.I file
]
.br
.B certify firstboot
.br
.B certify resign
.br
.B certify rotate
.br
.B certify activate
.br
.B certify show
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Certify
makes the files that Inferno's mutual authentication needs: a signer
key for the machine, one certificate per user, and the EdDSA signer and
epoch material used by
.IR monoauth (8)
and
.IR monoca (8).
.PP
The authentication protocol
.RB ( keyring-auth (2))
has each side send a certificate signed by a common signer, and prove
possession of the corresponding secret key.  It does not require the
signer to be on line: the signer's
.I public
key travels inside every certificate as
.BR spk ,
and each side verifies the other's certificate with it.
.I Certify
therefore needs no
.IR keyfs (4),
.IR signer (8)
or
.IR logind (8)
daemon.
.SS Init
.B certify init
creates
.B /keydb/signerkey
for
.IR owner
(default
.BR signer ).
The file holds the signer's private key and must be readable only by
root.  An existing key is never replaced: replacing it invalidates
every certificate already issued.
.SS User
.B certify user
.I name
writes a certificate for
.I name
to
.IR file ,
by default
.BR /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/default ,
creating the directory if necessary.
Each run generates a fresh RSA key pair; the certificate signs its
public half.  Running
.B certify user
again therefore makes a
.I different
certificate, and the one it replaces stays valid, because there is no
revocation: see
.SM BUGS .
.PP
.B \-f
allows an existing certificate to be replaced.
.PP
Give the file to the user; it is what
.I listen
reads on the server and what
.IR rcmd (1)
or
.IR cpu (1)
present on the client.  With
.IR rcmd ,
.B \-f
.IR file
selects it.
.SS Firstboot
.B certify firstboot
is what the PC kernel's init runs before starting
.IR svcd (8):
create the signer key if it is missing, then create a certificate for
the console user (the name in
.BR /dev/user )
if that is missing too.  This makes a freshly provisioned machine
usable without an operator, at the cost of a key that differs from
machine to machine.
.SS Mono signer
.B certify
also maintains the EdDSA signer used by
.IR monoauth (8)
and
.IR monoca (8).
A fresh key is written to
.B /keydb/mono-signer
and its public half to
.BR /keydb/mono-signer.pub .
When a key-encryption key is available
.RB ( \-k
.IR keyfile ,
or
.B /n/host/keydb/mono.key
when the host supplies one) the secret is encrypted at rest with
Argon2id and XChaCha20-Poly1305; see
.IR monosigner (2).
The public file is mode 644 so verifiers can read it; the secret is
mode 600.
.B \-k
names the key-encryption key for every command that touches the mono
signer; without it the host default is used, and without either the
secret is written in plaintext.
Because
.B firstboot
now publishes a first epoch with
.BR "monoca enroll" ,
a provisioned machine authenticates immediately and
.IR monoca (8)
renews the epoch from then on.
.SS Rotation
.B certify rotate
makes a new signer, stages it in
.BR /keydb/mono-signer.next ,
and adds its public key to the trusted list in
.BR mono-signer.pub .
Copy
.B /keydb/mono-signer.next.pub
to every node as
.B /keydb/mono-signer.pub
(or merge its pk line), then run
.B certify activate
on the issuing host.
.B activate
keeps the previous secret as
.BR /keydb/mono-signer.old ,
installs the staged one,
.B resigns
every local identity (the user's own key is unchanged; only the
certificate is re-signed), and runs
.B monoca publish
so the next epoch is signed by the new key.  Both keys stay trusted
until the old pk line is removed by hand.
.PP
.B certify resign
can also be run on its own after a rotation, or to re-sign identities
whose certificate was lost; identities on other hosts keep their old
certificate until the command is run there.
.SS Show
.B certify show
prints the RSA signer, the mono secret (and whether it is encrypted),
the public file, the current public key and every
.B /usr/*/keyring/default
it can find.
.SH SECURITY
The protocol is mutual: each side sends a certificate and proves
possession of the matching secret key, and the user name is part of the
signed public key, so a certificate cannot be relabelled.  That much was
verified.
.PP
What it is not:
.IP \(bu
A certificate is a
.I bearer token.
Its file also holds the private key.  Anyone who can read it can act as
that user.
.IP \(bu
One signer key per machine, never expiring, with no revocation list.
Replacing
.B /keydb/signerkey
invalidates every certificate; replacing one user's certificate does
not invalidate the one that user already has.
.IP \(bu
There are no real user identifiers inside an Inferno instance.
.B runas
writes
.BR /dev/user ,
and any process can write it.  Every process on the machine can also
read
.B /keydb/signerkey
and every certificate.  Treat the user name as accounting, not as a
security boundary; the boundary is the
.I cpud
allowlist.
.IP \(bu
.B \-a rc4_128
uses Inferno's SSL device, which is the SSLv2-era record layer with RC4
and MD5.  It stops casual sniffing on a LAN; it is not modern
confidentiality.  With
.B \-a none
the session is authenticated but completely in the clear, including the
certificate exchange.
.IP \(bu
The machine key created by
.B firstboot
is written into the root file system.  It is encrypted when the host
supplies
.BR /n/host/keydb/mono.key ,
but that file is itself the key: a clone of the image plus the key file
is a clone of the CA.  Clone an image
.I before
first boot, or every clone shares one signer key.
.IP \(bu
Rotation is a two-step operation for a reason: a node that has not
received the new public key rejects certificates and epochs signed by
it.  Distribute
.B mono-signer.pub
before
.BR activate .
.SH EXAMPLES
Create the machine key and a certificate for the console user:
.IP
.EX
; certify firstboot
certify: signer key for signer in /keydb/signerkey
certify: inferno -> /usr/inferno/keyring/default
.EE
.PP
Add a user, then run a command as that user:
.IP
.EX
; certify user alice
certify: alice -> /usr/alice/keyring/default
; rcmd -f /usr/alice/keyring/default -e rc4_128 tcp!server!6668 echo hello
hello
.EE
.PP
The server side needs the same command started through
.IR listen (1)
with authentication enabled, for example:
.IP
.EX
listen -a rc4_128 tcp!*!6668 /dis/sh.dis /lib/cpusession.sh
.EE
.PP
where
.B /lib/cpusession.sh
runs
.BI "runas $user /dis/cpud.dis" ;
.I listen
sets
.B $user
from the certificate the client presented.
.SH FILES
.TF /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/default
.TP
.B /keydb/signerkey
signer private key; root only
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer
EdDSA signer secret; encrypted when a key file is available; root only
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer.pub
trusted EdDSA public keys; mode 644
.TP
.BI /usr/ name /keyring/default
user certificate
.TP
.BI /usr/ name /keyring/mono
EdDSA identity: name, domain, path, secret key and certificate
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/certify.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR listen (1),
.IR rcmd (1),
.IR cpu (1),
.IR cpud (8),
.IR keyring-auth (2),
.IR createsignerkey (8),
.IR monoauth (8),
.IR monoca (8),
.IR monosigner (2)
.SH BUGS
To withdraw a user, delete the certificate file; that only helps if the
user has not kept a copy.  To withdraw everyone, replace
.B /keydb/signerkey
and reissue every certificate.  Nothing expires on its own.
.PP
See
.SM SECURITY
for what the mechanism does not provide.
